#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060510} :activity/id #uuid "106ffb37-ea9c-5d76-81d8-7461b589f327" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050242} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060890 :tutorial/content "The following two identities are known as the **sum and difference formulas for tangent**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (x + y) &= \\frac{\\tan x + \\tan y}{1 - \\tan x\\tan y} \\\\\n\\tan (x - y) &= \\frac{\\tan x - \\tan y}{1 + \\tan x\\tan y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the sum and difference rules to find the exact values of some trigonometric ratios. For example, let's use the sum rule to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\tan(75^\\circ)\n$$\n\nWe start by writing $75^\\circ$ as a sum of special angles:\n\n$$\n75^\\circ = {\\color{blue}45^\\circ} + {\\color{red}30^\\circ}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the sum formula for tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (75^{∘}) &= \\tan (30^{∘} + 45^{∘}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan 30^{∘} + \\tan 45^{∘}}{1 - \\tan 30^{∘}\\tan 45^{∘}} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + 1))/((1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cdot 1)) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + 1))/((1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (75^{∘}) &= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + 1))/((1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} + 1))/(3 \\cdot (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 3}{3 - \\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{3 - \\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (75^{∘}) &= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{3 - \\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{3 - \\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{3 + \\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= (3^{2} + 6\\sqrt{3} + (\\sqrt{3})^{2})/(3^{2} - (\\sqrt{3})^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9 + 6\\sqrt{3} + 3}{9 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 + 6\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\\n&= 2 + \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan (75^\\circ) = 2+\\sqrt 3\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "367c13cc-df0b-56b3-86ab-db2e2b6d8e98" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2561 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87477c37-0166-55b3-9132-57c8f2119a1f" :step/math-academy-id 11739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059664 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059495 :question/id #uuid "652143ae-b765-5abe-a7e9-a90ec15dd38f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3521" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(x-y) = \\dfrac{\\tan{x}-\\tan{y}}{1+\\tan{x}\\tan{y}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{12}\\right) &= \\tan (\\frac{π}{3} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= (\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right) - \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(1 + \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{3}\\right)\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{1 + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{12}\\right) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3} + (-1)^{2})/((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 1}{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b97e00a-b343-5b04-b475-3aaa038995ca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060280}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Exact Value Using the Sum and Difference Formulas for Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "e6556015-533b-577c-9fb4-08181a0e3e79" :step/math-academy-id 11740 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059667 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059498 :question/id #uuid "6fdd424e-5136-54b5-a2ef-c4d658597374" :question/math-academy-id "e-3525" :question/problem "Write $\\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ as an expression containing $\\tan{x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(u-v) = \\dfrac{\\tan{u}-\\tan{v}}{1+\\tan{u}\\tan{v}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with $u=x$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (x - \\frac{π}{4}) &= (\\tan x - \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(1 + \\tan x\\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{1 + \\tan x \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{\\tan x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b88d3f1-b190-54db-ac56-6f70e6475221" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676534} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676529}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Sum and Difference Formulas: Radians"} :step/id #uuid "c96e8302-dc4e-5eb6-b5f1-c57910d7d6c6" :step/math-academy-id 11817 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060957 :tutorial/content "We've practiced using the sum and difference formulas for tangent. But where do they come from?\n\nTo derive the sum formula for tangent, we start off by recalling the sum and difference formulas for sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (x + y) &= \\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y \\\\\n\\cos (x + y) &= \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe can divide $\\sin(x+ y)$ by $\\cos(x+ y)$ to get $\\tan(x+ y)$. Dividing, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin(x+ y)}{\\cos(x+ y)} = \\dfrac{\\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y}{\\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y}\n$$\n\nand simplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\tan(x+y) = \\dfrac{\\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y}{\\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y}\n$$\n\nTo write the right-hand side in terms of tangent only, we divide the numerator and denominator by $\\cos x\\cos y$. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\tan   ( x + y ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x \\cos   y + \\cos   x \\sin   y}{\\cos   x \\cos   y - \\sin   x \\sin   y}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x \\cos   y}{\\cos   x \\cos   y}} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   x \\sin   y}{\\cos   x \\cos   y}} )}{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   x \\cos   y}{\\cos   x \\cos   y}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x \\sin   y}{\\cos   x \\cos   y}} )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   y}}{\\cos   x \\cancel{\\cos   y}}} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\cos   x} \\sin   y}{\\cancel{\\cos   x} \\cos   y}} )}{( 1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x \\sin   y}{\\cos   x \\cos   y}} )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x}{\\cos   x}} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   y}{\\cos   y}} )}{( 1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   x}{\\cos   x}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   y}{\\cos   y}} )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\tan   x + \\tan   y}{1 - \\tan   x \\tan   y}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWe can derive the difference formula for tangent in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea32c1b4-9316-5da5-9075-239dcda6e2ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4146 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Sum and Difference Formulas for Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "d894c19a-cbf9-54d6-add0-21fd6d1b9615" :step/math-academy-id 25194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059629 :question/id #uuid "79cf1bcf-917c-5727-9bc3-14e5402b5f4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9993" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac {4\\sqrt 3} 3$, then find the value of $\\tan (x -30^\\circ)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(u-v) = \\dfrac{\\tan{u}-\\tan{v}}{1+\\tan{u}\\tan{v}}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for tangent with $u=x$ and\n\n$$\nv=30^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\tan(x -30^\\circ) &= \\dfrac{\\tan x - \\tan 30^\\circ}{1 + \\tan x \\tan 30^\\circ} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{4 \\sqrt 3}3 -\\dfrac{1} {\\sqrt 3}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\left(-\\dfrac{4 \\sqrt 3}3\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac {4\\sqrt 3} 3 - \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3} {\\sqrt 3}\\right)} {\\left(1-\\dfrac 43\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac {4\\sqrt 3} 3 - \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3} {3}\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 13\\right)} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {\\left(-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 1 3 \\right)} \\\\[3pt] & = 5\\sqrt{3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d1fea0-4383-513f-a266-0b2b49d4332e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677312} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677309}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Sum and Difference Formulas: Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "85d6c4f7-a4e9-5789-aa9e-44b9b31597eb" :step/math-academy-id 37644 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060515}}] :activity/title "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Tangent" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059947}]}
